\chapter{Entities}
\label{ch:entities}

An \emph{entity} is anything in the world the player can act on: a character to
talk to, an item to pick up, a horse that wanders.  Following the Overview
(\cref{ch:overview}), all of these are one base structure rather than a
hierarchy of separate character and item classes --- a horse is simply an
entity that talks and cannot be taken, a key one that can.

Where this chapter earns its design is in separating three things that pull in
different directions:

\begin{description}
  \item[Content] is \emph{data} --- an entity's name, description, and the
    lines it speaks.  It is authored, translated, and eventually loaded from a
    swappable on-disk database, so it must stay inert and serialisable.
  \item[Behaviour] is \emph{code} --- how an entity responds to being looked
    at, talked to, used, or taken.  Canned text covers the simple cases, but a
    gatekeeper who poses a riddle needs a real method, so behaviour lives in
    overridable methods that subclasses can specialise.
  \item[State] is \emph{neither} --- whether a conversation has already
    happened, what the player did last time.  It is mutable and must survive
    between runs, so it belongs to the saved game state --- owned by the loop
    and persistence chapters that come later --- keyed by an entity's stable
    [[id]], not stored on the entity itself.
\end{description}

Keeping these apart is what lets one entity be cheap data, gain coded behaviour
by subclassing, and remember the player across sessions, without any one
concern entangling the others.  This chapter builds the content-and-behaviour
half: an immutable [[Entity]] whose four interaction methods return
\emph{events} for a frontend to render, exactly as the engine does
(\cref{ch:game}).  The state half arrives with the loop and persistence that
own it.

The module gathers the usual buckets.  Its events come from the engine chapter
and its language helpers from the package root, so those are the only imports.

<<[[entities.py]]>>=
"""World entities --- one structure for characters and items.

An Entity carries immutable content (name, description, dialogue) and answers
the player's interactions (look/talk/use/take) by returning events.  Simple
entities are pure data; richer ones subclass and override an interaction.
This module imports no user interface and calls neither ``print`` nor
``input``.
"""

<<imports>>

<<constants>>

<<classes>>
@

<<imports>>=
from dataclasses import dataclass

from maventyr import DEFAULT_LANGUAGE, localize
from maventyr.game import Message
@

\section{The entity}

An entity is a frozen dataclass: its content never changes once authored, and
freezing says so in the type.  The fields are the content the game and the
on-disk database supply; the methods, defined in the sections that follow, are
the behaviour.

The [[id]] comes first and carries no display role --- it is the stable handle
by which everything else refers to this entity: the location that holds it, the
saved game state that records what the player has done with it, and the content
database that will one day construct it.  [[name]] is the short label shown when
an entity is listed or taken; [[description]] is what looking at it reveals.
The remaining fields are optional because they distinguish a character from an
item purely by data: a thing with [[dialogue]] is someone to talk to, a thing
that is [[takeable]] is something to pick up, and a thing with a
[[use_response]] is something to use.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Entity:
    """A character or item the player can interact with.

    Holds immutable, localizable content; the look/talk/use/take methods turn
    that content into events.  Subclass to give an entity coded behaviour
    beyond its canned responses.
    """

    id: str
    name: dict
    description: dict
    dialogue: dict | None = None
    use_response: dict | None = None
    takeable: bool = False

    <<entity methods>>
@

\subsection{Looking}

Looking is the one interaction every entity supports, because every entity has
a description.  There is nothing to decide --- return the description,
localized.

<<entity methods>>=
def look(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the entity's description as an event for the player."""
    return [Message(localize(self.description, language))]
@

We return a one-element list rather than a bare event so every interaction
shares the engine's command\,$\to$\,events shape: a richer entity can later
answer with several events without changing the interface.

To keep the tests below from restating an entity's content each time, we build
them from a small helper and override only the field under test.  Let's verify
a look reads the description back in the asked-for language:

<<test [[entities.py]]>>=
from maventyr.entities import *
from maventyr.game import Event


def _thing(**overrides):
    fields = dict(
        id="thing",
        name={"sv": "saken", "en": "the thing"},
        description={"sv": "En vanlig sak.", "en": "An ordinary thing."},
    )
    fields.update(overrides)
    return Entity(**fields)


def test_look_returns_description():
    thing = _thing()
    assert thing.look("sv") == [Message(thing.description["sv"])]
    assert thing.look("en") == [Message(thing.description["en"])]
@

\subsection{Talking}

Talking is the first interaction that can come up empty: an item has nothing to
say.  We treat an absent [[dialogue]] not as an error but as a quiet entity, and
answer with a shared fallback line.  This is the safe default that makes
subclassing cheap later --- a subclass overrides [[talk]] only when it has
something more interesting to say than \enquote{nothing}.

<<constants>>=
NO_RESPONSE = {
    "sv": "Den säger ingenting.",
    "en": "It says nothing.",
}
@

<<entity methods>>=
def talk(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return what the entity says, or a fallback if it has no dialogue."""
    if self.dialogue is None:
        return [Message(localize(NO_RESPONSE, language))]
    return [Message(localize(self.dialogue, language))]
@

A character returns its line; a thing with no dialogue returns the fallback:

<<test [[entities.py]]>>=
def test_talk_returns_dialogue_when_present():
    horse = _thing(dialogue={"sv": "Gnägg!", "en": "Neigh!"})
    assert horse.talk("sv") == [Message("Gnägg!")]


def test_talk_falls_back_when_silent():
    assert _thing().talk("sv") == [Message(NO_RESPONSE["sv"])]
@

\subsection{Taking}

Taking is the only interaction that wants to change the world --- the item
should leave the room and join the player.  But there is no room and no
inventory yet (\cref{ch:overview} places them in later chapters), so here
[[take]] only reports the outcome; the actual move waits for the world that
holds one.  What it \emph{can} decide now, from data alone, is whether the
attempt is even allowed: [[takeable]] is what separates a key from a horse.

<<constants>>=
CANNOT_TAKE = {
    "sv": "Du kan inte ta {name}.",
    "en": "You can't take {name}.",
}
TAKEN = {
    "sv": "Du tar {name}.",
    "en": "You take {name}.",
}
@

The responses name the entity so the sentence reads naturally; the [[name]]
field is authored as a noun phrase that drops into the template
(\enquote{Du tar nyckeln}).

<<entity methods>>=
def take(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the result of trying to take the entity.

    Reports success or refusal only; moving it into an inventory waits for
    the world that has one.
    """
    name = localize(self.name, language)
    template = TAKEN if self.takeable else CANNOT_TAKE
    return [Message(localize(template, language).format(name=name))]
@

A takeable thing is taken; anything else is refused, in the player's language:

<<test [[entities.py]]>>=
def test_take_succeeds_when_takeable():
    key = _thing(name={"sv": "nyckeln", "en": "the key"}, takeable=True)
    assert key.take("sv") == [Message("Du tar nyckeln.")]


def test_take_refused_when_not_takeable():
    horse = _thing(name={"sv": "hästen", "en": "the horse"})
    assert horse.take("en") == [Message("You can't take the horse.")]
@

\subsection{Using}

Using mirrors talking: most entities do nothing in particular when used, and
those that do carry a [[use_response]].  The same safe-default shape keeps the
door open for subclasses whose use has real effects later.

<<constants>>=
NOTHING_HAPPENS = {
    "sv": "Inget händer.",
    "en": "Nothing happens.",
}
@

<<entity methods>>=
def use(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the entity's use response, or a fallback if it has none."""
    if self.use_response is None:
        return [Message(localize(NOTHING_HAPPENS, language))]
    return [Message(localize(self.use_response, language))]
@

<<test [[entities.py]]>>=
def test_use_returns_response_when_present():
    wand = _thing(use_response={"sv": "Det gnistrar.", "en": "It sparkles."})
    assert wand.use("en") == [Message("It sparkles.")]


def test_use_falls_back_when_inert():
    assert _thing().use("sv") == [Message(NOTHING_HAPPENS["sv"])]
@

Whatever the interaction, it returns a list of events --- the shape the engine
and both frontends rely on:

<<test [[entities.py]]>>=
def test_interactions_return_lists_of_events():
    thing = _thing()
    for events in (thing.look(), thing.talk(), thing.take(), thing.use()):
        assert isinstance(events, list)
        assert all(isinstance(event, Event) for event in events)
@

\section{Behaviour by subclassing}

Canned responses only reach so far: a character who poses a riddle, grades an
answer, or remembers the player needs real code.  Because behaviour lives in
methods, that case needs no new machinery --- subclass [[Entity]] and override
the one interaction that differs.  The other three keep their safe defaults, so
a subclass is never obliged to reimplement what it does not change, and adding a
future interaction to the base will not break it.  This is the primary way the
game will grow interesting entities; the canned defaults above are the floor it
builds on.%
\ltnote{%
  We present subclassing last, after all four canned interactions, so the reader
  has already watched every safe default in action.  Overriding then reads as
  \enquote{replace one of these} rather than as an abstract extension point met
  before the behaviour it replaces.
}

We verify the pattern with a gatekeeper that answers talk with its own coded
line, and confirm that an interaction it did \emph{not} override still falls
back to the safe default:

<<test [[entities.py]]>>=
def test_subclass_overrides_one_interaction():
    class Gatekeeper(Entity):
        CHALLENGE = {"sv": "Vem där?", "en": "Who goes there?"}

        def talk(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
            return [Message(localize(self.CHALLENGE, language))]

    guard = Gatekeeper(
        id="gatekeeper",
        name={"sv": "väktaren", "en": "the gatekeeper"},
        description={"sv": "En sträng väktare.", "en": "A stern gatekeeper."},
    )

    assert guard.talk("sv") == [Message("Vem där?")]

    # an un-overridden interaction keeps the base's safe default:
    refused = localize(CANNOT_TAKE, "sv").format(name="väktaren")
    assert guard.take("sv") == [Message(refused)]
@
